{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01VE6Vzkv5CvAbwayvP6YXWF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Java 25! That's the problem. Spring Framework 4.3.4 uses an old version of CGLIB/ASM that cannot generate bytecode for Java 9+ class format. Specifically, the ASM bundled with Spring 4.3.4 cannot handle Java 9+ class files.\n\nThe existing unit tests work because they use standalone MockMvc or Mockito — they don't need Spring's @Configuration class enhancement (CGLIB). But when you load a Spring @Configuration class via @ContextConfiguration, Spring uses CGLIB to create a subclass of your @Configuration class, and that fails on Java 22/25.\n \nI'm thinking through the workarounds here. The core issue is that @Configuration triggers CGLIB enhancement, which fails with the old ASM. I could switch to XML-based test configuration instead, or use @ContextConfiguration with factory methods to avoid the CGLIB proxy generation entirely. There's also the option of disabling proxy bean methods if we upgrade to Spring 5.2+, but that's a bigger change than just adjusting how we configure the test context.\n\nWait, but I'm noticing something — other tests in the codebase are passing fine. The ones using MockitoJUnitRunner don't need Spring at all, and the tests using standaloneSetup() for MockMvc bypass the full Spring context initialization, so they never hit the CGLIB issue. That's the pattern I should follow for the failing test.\n\nThe real problem surfaces when I load a Spring application context through `@ContextConfiguration(classes = TestAppConfig.class)` — that's what triggers CGLIB to enhance the `@Configuration` class, and that's where Java 25's module restrictions are causing the failure.\n\nI could switch to XML-based configuration to avoid CGLIB entirely, but that's a bigger refactor. Or I could try adding JVM arguments to the surefire plugin to work around the module restrictions, though the root cause seems to be an ASM version incompatibility.\n\nActually, I'm realizing the issue is more fundamental: Spring 4.3.4 bundles ASM 5.x, which only understands Java 8 class files (version 52), but Java 22 generates class version 66. When ASM tries to process these newer class files during CGLIB enhancement, it fails to initialize the Enhancer class itself. Using `--release 8` during test compilation would generate older bytecode that ASM can actually handle. files compatible with Java 8. But ASM still reads the Enhancer class itself which is compiled for newer Java...wait, that's not quite right either. ASM doesn't read the Enhancer class, it generates bytecode.\n\nActually wait - let me re-read. The error is:\n```\nCaused by: java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError: Could not initialize class org.springframework.cglib.proxy.Enhancer\n```\n\nThis means the `Enhancer` class **itself** could not be initialized. When a class fails static initialization, you get `NoClassDefFoundError` or `ExceptionInInitializerError` on subsequent attempts to use that class. \n\nThe Enhancer class has static initializer that uses CGLIB features... and one of the nested classes may call asm to verify something. The ASM library may fail when it encounters Java 22 class format.\n\nSo the real issue is that CGLIB's LoadingCache tries to generate a bytecode-based cache key factory during static initialization, and ASM 5 can't handle the newer class file format that gets generated.\n\nOption A won't work because the problem is in the static initialization of Enhancer itself, not in the test compilation.\n\nOption B: Upgrade Spring to a version that bundles a newer ASM that understands Java 22 class files.\n\nOption C: Use a Java version that matches what Spring 4.3.4 was designed for (Java 8-11 range).\n\nOption B seems most viable if we can upgrade Spring.\n\nActually, wait — I need to reconsider the root cause. CGLIB generates new bytecode, and the bytecode version it outputs depends on what ASM can generate. ASM 5 maxes out at Java 8 bytecode (version 52), while Java 9+ requires ASM 6+. But here's the thing: CGLIB doesn't need to generate Java 22 bytecode — it can generate Java 8 bytecode that runs fine on Java 22 since Java is backward compatible.\n\nThe real problem is that CGLIB/ASM reads the TestAppConfig class file to understand what methods to proxy, and when it encounters a Java 22 class file (version 66), ASM 5 fails to parse it. So the compilation target of TestAppConfig is what matters — if it's compiled with `--target 8`, the class file would be version 52 and ASM 5 could read it.\n\nLet me look at the Maven compiler configuration to see what target version is being used.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":631,"cache_read_input_tokens":61069,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":631},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6547310c-c57f-4bb9-bd57-530175104ef3","uuid":"e746b93a-8725-4920-b37e-a7ca09945197"}
