--add-opens java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED` to allow reflection access, or leveraging objenesis (which Spring already includes) to bypass CGLIB proxy generation entirely. I could try the JVM flags in the surefire plugin configuration, or I could simplify the test configuration class so it doesn't need to be a full proxy-aware `@Configuration` if there are no interdependent `@Bean` methods.\n\nActually, in Spring 4 there's no declarative way to avoid CGLIB enhancement on `@Configuration` classes, but I could switch to `@Component` instead—Spring will still process `@Bean` methods on component-annotated classes without the CGLIB proxy overhead. The trade-off is that direct method calls between beans would create new instances instead of returning singletons, but I can work around that by injecting the dependencies as method parameters rather than calling other bean methods directly.\n\nLooking at the test config, the methods are calling `dataSource()` directly from within other bean methods, which is exactly why `@Configuration` exists. But I don't actually need those direct calls—I can refactor to inject `DataSource` as a parameter instead, which is the proper Spring pattern and would work fine with `@Component`.\n\nRefactoring the bean methods to accept DataSource as a parameter...\n\nThis approach eliminates the inter-bean method calls entirely, so the CGLIB proxy requirement goes away. Whether I use `@Configuration` or `@Component`, Spring will inject the same singleton `DataSource` instance into each method, and everything works correctly. I'm realizing that `@EnableTransactionManagement` relies on infrastructure beans that need proper `@Configuration` registration, so switching to `@Component` might break that. Instead of fighting CGLIB limitations, I could add the `--add-opens` JVM argument to the surefire configuration to allow the reflection that CGLIB needs, which would be a simpler fix than restructuring the config class. The `--add-opens` approach is cleaner than refactoring the code, so I'll add those JVM arguments to the surefire plugin configuration. But I'm realizing the real issue here—CGLIB's use of `Unsafe.defineAnonymousClass()` was removed in Java 17, and the proper fix requires using `MethodHandles.Lookup.defineClass()` instead, which means I need to check if Spring 4.3 even supports that or if I need a different approach entirely. The `ExceptionInInitializerError` is happening in the `Enhancer` class's static initializer, which is trying to set up field updaters and cache mechanisms. When `LoadingCache` attempts to generate a new class, it's likely hitting a reflection or bytecode generation issue that's being masked by the initialization error.\n\nThe real problem is that CGLIB's `KeyFactory` dynamically generates classes using ASM bytecode, then tries to define them via reflection on `ClassLoader.defineClass()`. In Java 9+, this reflection call is blocked by module restrictions unless you explicitly allow it with `--add-opens java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED`. Spring 4.3.x's CGLIB doesn't have support for the newer `MethodHandles.Lookup.defineClass()` approach, so the workaround is to add that JVM flag.\n\nI should add both `--add-opens java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED` and `--add-opens java.base/java.lang.reflect=ALL-UNNAMED` to the surefire plugin's argLine configuration to handle Spring 4 on Java 17+. But I'm weighing whether modifying the pom.xml is the cleanest solution versus refactoring `TestAppConfig` to avoid requiring CGLIB enhancement in the first place.\n\nOption A—adding the JVM flags—is the minimal fix that preserves the existing code structure, though it does change the build configuration. Option B would involve converting `@Configuration` to `@Component` and using parameter injection instead, which addresses the root cause but requires more code changes and is complicated in Spring 4 without `proxyBeanMethods = false`. I think Option A is the right call here since the CGLIB issue is fundamentally a JVM compatibility problem, not a design flaw, and adding `--add-opens` is the standard approach for running Spring 4.x on Java 17.\n\nWait, let me look at that error message again—the `ExceptionInInitializerError` has a null cause, which is odd. That suggests the static initializer caught an exception but didn't properly rethrow it, which could mean the reflection access is failing silently when ASM tries to use `sun.misc.Unsafe.defineClass()` on a modern JVM. I'm wondering if the real issue is how `LoadingCache.createEntry` wraps exceptions, which might be masking the actual problem.\n\nIf `e.getCause()` is null when creating the `IllegalStateException`, that would explain why we're seeing this null cause in the stack trace. The actual failure is happening inside the `FutureTask` that wraps the ASM class generation. I think the solution is to add JVM module access flags like `--add-opens java.base/java.lang=ALL-UNNAMED` and possibly `--add-opens java.base/sun.reflect=ALL-UNNAMED` to the surefire configuration to allow the reflection access that ASM needs.\n\nCGLIB specifically uses `ClassLoader.defineClass()` through reflection, which requires opening the `java.lang` module. Let me add this fix and test it, though first I should check if there are any existing `JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS` or `MAVEN_OPTS` environment variables that might already be affecting this.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6270,"cache_read_input_tokens":66863,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6270},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6547310c-c57f-4bb9-bd57-530175104ef3","uuid":"937f413d-3af1-4e90-b0e2-7409f46a72bc"}
